      "turn": 12,
      "doctor_question": "Before age 12, did you often have trouble paying attention, staying organized, or keeping track of things?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, yeah. I mean, my mom actually brought this up recently when I was telling her about the whole mess at work. She was like, \"You know you were exactly like this back in grade school, right?\" And honestly, she's not wrong. I remember it being a whole thing. Like, I was always the kid who just could not stay in his seat. The teacher would be up there talking, and I'd be up wandering over to the pencil sharpener, or checking out the class pet, or just standing up at my desk for no reason. \n\nAnd the blurting out answers... man, that drove my teachers crazy. I just couldn't wait to be called on. The thought would pop into my head and boom, it was out of my mouth before I even realized my hand wasn't raised. And the homework... don't even get me started on the homework. It wasn't that I couldn't do it. I was a smart kid, you know? I grasped the material fine. But I'd do the worksheet, shove it in my desk or my backpack, and then the next day when it was time to turn it in? Gone. Vanished. It was like a black hole in my desk. \n\nMy mom got so many notes sent home. I mean, constantly. \"He's bright, but he doesn't apply himself,\" or \"He needs to focus,\" or \"He's disrupting the class.\" It was always something. She tried color-coded folders, checklists on the fridge, everything. Nothing really stuck. I just kind of scraped by because I could pass the tests, but the day-to-day stuff, the organization, just keeping my head on straight... it was a disaster even back then. So, yeah, it's definitely not a new thing, even if it feels like it's ruining my life more now than it did when I was nine.",
